e.\\n\\n## The backfill is only exercised by a database that predates the migration\\n\\n`prisma/seed.ts` creates no bookings, and dev and CI databases are built from empty by\\n`migrate deploy` or `migrate reset`. So when the \\\"has an entry for every booking\\\"\\nassertion runs, the only bookings present are ones the trigger already covered:\\ndeleting the backfill `INSERT` from the migration leaves the suite green everywhere it\\nnormally runs.\\n\\nThe verifier did exercise it by hand — a pre-migration database holding two bookings,\\nmigrated over, produced exactly one history row each with `from` null and the booking's\\nown `createdAt`, zero orphans — and the guard was confirmed in the same way, rejecting\\n`'REFUNDED'` and `'pending'` and rolling back whole.\\n\\nThe assertion is kept because the invariant it protects is real going forward. The gap\\nis that the only database where the backfill matters is a deployed one, which nothing\\ntests against. Worth remembering for any later migration in this issue that backfills.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T03:59:38Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5235702982\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOJZgAw\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"## What slice 4 covers, and what is left of this issue's task list\\n\\nSlice 4 delivers the staff side and the refund half of the customer's choice: a staff\\nflight cancellation moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED`, the seat stays held, the customer\\ncan take a full refund, and reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\n**Rebooking is not in it, and should be its own slice.** \\\"Offer rebooking, refund, or\\ncredit for disrupted itineraries\\\" needs a flight search constrained to the same route and\\na comparable date, seat reselection on the new flight, a fare difference to resolve, and\\na decision about what happens to the original booking's history — none of which is a\\nsmall addition to the profile row. Building a fraction of it alongside the refund would\\nhave made both worse.\\n\\nCredit is also untouched: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and #75\\nowns money.\\n\\nSo what remains on this issue after slice 5 (end-to-end coverage):\\n\\n- Rebooking a disrupted itinerary onto another flight.\\n- Credit as an alternative to a refund, if it is wanted at all once #75 lands.\\n\\nBoth are worth their own issues rather than lingering here; happy to file them when the\\nslices are done, or fold rebooking into a Phase 4 customer-support item, whichever you\\nprefer.\\n\\n## One deliberate gap in the disruption path\\n\\n`lib/PointsActivityService.ts` keys every branch off `=== 'CANCELLED'`, so a `DISRUPTED`\\nbooking still earns status points and still shows as an ordinary credit. That is the\\nright behaviour — the customer has not cancelled and may yet fly a rebooked itinerary —\\nbut nothing tests it, and it will need revisiting when rebooking lands.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T18:30:30Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5244346371\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOKZr9A\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"## All five slices are merged\\n\\n| Slice | PR | What it did |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | #228 | `Booking.status` typed as an enum; an append-only status history that writes itself |\\n| 2 | #230 | Seats released by saying so, not by overwriting the seat number |\\n| 3 | #231 | The cancellation policy: eligibility, fees, refunds recorded |\\n| 4 | #232 | A staff flight cancellation disrupts its bookings, and can be undone |\\n| 5 | #233 | Browser journeys for the self-service and staff-assisted paths |\\n\\nAgainst this issue's original task list:\\n\\n- **Cancellation eligibility by fare and departure time** — done. Refused once departed;\\n  First and Business refund in full before the 24-hour cut-off, Premium Economy and\\n  Economy less a fifth; inside the cut-off cancellation is still allowed at nothing back.\\n- **Release seats without rewriting seat identifiers** — done. A partial unique index\\n  covers held seats only, so a released row keeps the seat the traveller actually sat in.\\n- **Calculate and record refunds** — done, on the history row, via a transaction-local\\n  channel. No money moves: #75 owns that and will have a figure to refund against.\\n- **Handle airline-initiated cancellations** — done, as `DISRUPTED`, reversibly.\\n- **Preserve immutable booking and status history** — done, enforced by a trigger rather\\n  than by convention.\\n- **Offer rebooking, refund or credit** — the refund only. Rebooking is now #234; credit\\n  is folded in there and probably depends on #75.\\n\\nAcceptance criteria: the changes are transactional and auditable, customer, inventory and\\nnotification states stay consistent, and the two journeys are covered end to end.\\n\\n## Worth knowing before this is called finished\\n\\n**#84 undercuts the departure guard.** `Flight.departureDate` is local wall-clock time\\nlabelled UTC, so east of UTC a departed flight stays cancellable for the length of the\\norigin's offset — the exact case slice 3 closes. The policy is exact against the value it\\nis given; the value is not. Recorded on #84.\\n\\n**#229 and #208 together hide this feature on a phone.** The Status column is scrolled out\\nof the visible area and the notification drawer renders behind the page, so a disrupted\\nbooking looks identical to a confirmed one and the fallback channel is unreadable.\\nNeither is caused by this work; both are now load-bearing for it.\\n\\nLeaving this open rather than closing it, since #234 came out of it and the milestone is\\nyours to call.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T20:09:40Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5245398004\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true}],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":76,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.4 Implement cancellation, disruption, and refund rules\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76\"}\n"},"started_at_ms":1786902673569,"completed_at_ms":1786902673886}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T17:51:14.187Z","ordinal":23529,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"019ffe5b-1054-7c22-8afc-e97cb84c7b73","turn_id":"01a00bb2-2acf-7d53-ab0c-ded966187af9","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6d897a19-dc58-49e5-8eae-f6f237c0604a","process_id":"26444","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","gh issue view 77 --json number,title,body,state,labels,milestone,comments,url"],"cwd":"file:///home/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/travel-app","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh issue view 77 --json number,title,body,state,labels,milestone,comments,url"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"body\":\"Migrated from `REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md`.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\n- [ ] Add check-in eligibility windows and restrictions.\\n- [ ] Confirm traveler/document details at check-in.\\n- [ ] Support seat confirmation or allowed seat changes.\\n- [ ] Generate a boarding pass for each eligible traveler and leg.\\n- [ ] Provide confirmation numbers, receipts, and resend actions.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- The Check-In navigation leads to a complete journey.\\n- Ineligible customers receive a clear reason and next step.\\n- Boarding passes are scoped to the authenticated booking owner.\\n\",\"comments\":[],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":77,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.5 Implement check-in and travel documents\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/77\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"body\":\"Migrated from `REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md`.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\n- [ ] Add check-in eligibility windows and restrictions.\\n- [ ] Confirm traveler/document details at check-in.\\n- [ ] Support seat confirmation or allowed seat changes.\\n- [ ] Generate a boarding pass for each eligible traveler and leg.\\n- [ ] Provide confirmation numbers, receipts, and resend actions.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- The Check-In navigation leads to a complete journey.\\n- Ineligible customers receive a clear reason and next step.\\n- Boarding passes are scoped to the authenticated booking owner.\\n\",\"comments\":[],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":77,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.5 Implement check-in and travel documents\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/77\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":245148924},"formatted_output":"{\"body\":\"Migrated from `REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md`.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\n- [ ] Add check-in eligibility windows and restrictions.\\n- [ ] Confirm traveler/document details at check-in.\\n- [ ] Support seat confirmation or allowed seat changes.\\n- [ ] Generate a boarding pass for each eligible traveler and leg.\\n- [ ] Provide confirmation numbers, receipts, and resend actions.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- The Check-In navigation leads to a complete journey.\\n- Ineligible customers receive a clear reason and next step.\\n- Boarding passes are scoped to the authenticated booking owner.\\n\",\"comments\":[],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":77,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.5 Implement check-in and travel documents\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/77\"}\n"},"started_at_ms":1786902673941,"completed_at_ms":1786902674187}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T17:51:14.356Z","ordinal":23530,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a00bb2-fbb4-7e52-b4f7-d02ec2bc0f7a","call_id":"call_R1tMvthmUsNFFSQm89evwyVW","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 4.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"ISSUE 76\n{\"body\":\"Migrated from `REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md`.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\n- [ ] Define cancellation eligibility by fare and departure time.\\n- [ ] Release seats without rewriting seat identifiers.\\n- [ ] Calculate and record refunds or credits.\\n- [ ] Handle airline-initiated delays and cancellations.\\n- [ ] Offer rebooking, refund, or credit for disrupted itineraries.\\n- [ ] Preserve immutable booking and status history.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- Cancellation and disruption changes are transactional and auditable.\\n- Customer, inventory, payment, and notification states remain consistent.\\n- End-to-end tests cover self-service and staff-assisted paths.\\n\",\"comments\":[{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOA_LTw\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"## Decisions, and what is already here\\n\\nRecording the two answers this issue was waiting on, plus what reading the code first\\nturned up — because this is not greenfield.\\n\\n### What already ships\\n\\n`app/actions.ts:428` cancels a booking transactionally today: it locks the flights in\\nascending order, releases the seats, sets the status and notifies. What it has no\\nconcept of is **eligibility**. So two of this issue's tasks are live defects rather\\nthan missing features:\\n\\n- A customer can cancel **after the flight departed** — releasing a seat on a flown\\n  flight and deducting status points for a trip they took.\\n- Staff can set a flight to `CANCELLED` (`app/actions.ts:986`) and the bookings **stay\\n  `CONFIRMED`, still holding their seats**. The only consequence is a notification, so\\n  the customer keeps a boarding pass for a flight that is not operating.\\n\\nBoth read from the source; neither is confirmed against a running app yet.\\n\\nAlso relevant to the tasks above: `Booking.status` is a bare `String` with no enum and\\nno check constraint (`lib/seatOccupancy.ts:27` says so, and that literal is the whole\\ndefinition), and seats are released by renaming them to `CANCELLED-<passengerId>` to\\ndodge the unique index — which is why `lib/bookingItinerary.ts:82` has to translate the\\nplaceholder back into the word \\\"Released\\\" for display.\\n\\n### Cancellation policy\\n\\n| Cabin | Before 24h | Inside 24h | After departure |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| First, Business | Full refund | Cancel, no refund | Refused |\\n| Premium Economy, Economy | Refund minus 20% | Cancel, no refund | Refused |\\n| Any, flight `DISRUPTED` | Full refund, no fee, no cut-off | | |\\n\\n- **Percentage, not flat.** `Booking.currency` already exists, so a flat fee is wrong\\n  the moment a booking is not in USD and needs FX to stay right. A percentage of\\n  `totalPriceCents` needs neither. Rounding is floor, in minor units, stated here so\\n  tests pin it rather than inheriting whatever the implementation did first.\\n- **Cancellation stays allowed inside the cut-off, at zero refund.** Refusing strands\\n  the customer with a booking they cannot act on and keeps a seat held that could be\\n  resold.\\n- **Refused after departure** — an integrity rule more than a policy one. Cancelling a\\n  flown flight rewrites history, corrupts the points ledger and frees a seat that was\\n  used.\\n- Every cabin stays refundable. A non-refundable Economy would mean the most common\\n  path never exercises the refund calculation, leaving the e2e coverage thinnest where\\n  the traffic is heaviest.\\n\\n### A staff-cancelled flight moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED`\\n\\nNot `CANCELLED`. Auto-cancelling forecloses the rebooking this issue's own acceptance\\ncriteria ask for, releases seats that may be wanted for that rebooking, deducts status\\npoints for something the customer did not do, and is a one-way door — a misclick then\\nreversed leaves bookings destroyed and seats resold.\\n\\nKeeping the paths distinct is also what buys the rule that matters: **an involuntary\\ncancellation refunds in full, bypassing the fare rules and the cut-off**, because the\\nairline caused it. That is not expressible if both paths converge on one status.\\n\\nIf one leg of a round trip is cancelled, the whole booking goes `DISRUPTED`. Half an\\nitinerary is not a usable trip.\\n\\n### Money\\n\\n#75 (real payments) lands after this issue, so this one **calculates and records** the\\nrefund — an amount plus an immutable status-history row — and does not attempt a money\\nmovement. That keeps this shippable standalone and gives #75 a recorded figure to\\nrefund against.\\n\\n### Slices\\n\\n1. Type `Booking.status` as an enum and add the status-history table. Behaviour-neutral; #74 and #75 are both waiting on it.\\n2. Replace the `CANCELLED-<passengerId>` rename with `releasedAt` and a partial unique index — \\\"release seats without rewriting seat identifiers\\\", which also deletes the display hack.\\n3. Eligibility and refund calculation.\\n4. Staff cancel to `DISRUPTED`, and the customer's refund-or-rebook choice.\\n5. End-to-end coverage for the self-service and staff-assisted paths.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T03:24:47Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5235526479\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOBJ8xg\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"Two findings from slice 1's verification that belong to later slices rather than to it.\\n\\n## Two changes in one transaction share a timestamp, with no tiebreaker\\n\\n`record_booking_status_change` stamps `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP`, which is transaction start\\ntime, so three status changes in one transaction all carry the same instant:\\n\\n```\\n from      | to        | createdAt\\n           | CONFIRMED | 2026-08-10 03:53:34.791\\n CONFIRMED | DISRUPTED | 2026-08-10 03:53:34.791\\n DISRUPTED | CANCELLED | 2026-08-10 03:53:34.791\\n```\\n\\n`@@index([bookingId, createdAt])` cannot order those, and `id` is a random uuid, so\\n\\\"the latest status change\\\" is not answerable by `ORDER BY`. The order is recoverable\\nonly by chaining `from` to `to`.\\n\\nNothing does two status changes in one transaction today, so this is harmless now.\\n**Slice 3 records a refund against a history row and slice 5 shows a timeline** — both\\nwill want a monotonic column. Adding one is a small migration; noting it here so it is\\na decision rather than a rediscovery. Verified against running code.\\n\\n## The backfill is only exercised by a database that predates the migration\\n\\n`prisma/seed.ts` creates no bookings, and dev and CI databases are built from empty by\\n`migrate deploy` or `migrate reset`. So when the \\\"has an entry for every booking\\\"\\nassertion runs, the only bookings present are ones the trigger already covered:\\ndeleting the backfill `INSERT` from the migration leaves the suite green everywhere it\\nnormally runs.\\n\\nThe verifier did exercise it by hand — a pre-migration database holding two bookings,\\nmigrated over, produced exactly one history row each with `from` null and the booking's\\nown `createdAt`, zero orphans — and the guard was confirmed in the same way, rejecting\\n`'REFUNDED'` and `'pending'` and rolling back whole.\\n\\nThe assertion is kept because the invariant it protects is real going forward. The gap\\nis that the only database where the backfill matters is a deployed one, which nothing\\ntests against. Worth remembering for any later migration in this issue that backfills.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T03:59:38Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5235702982\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOJZgAw\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"## What slice 4 covers, and what is left of this issue's task list\\n\\nSlice 4 delivers the staff side and the refund half of the customer's choice: a staff\\nflight cancellation moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED`, the seat stays held, the customer\\ncan take a full refund, and reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\n**Rebooking is not in it, and should be its own slice.** \\\"Offer rebooking, refund, or\\ncredit for disrupted itineraries\\\" needs a flight search constrained to the same route and\\na comparable date, seat reselection on the new flight, a fare difference to resolve, and\\na decision about what happens to the original booking's history — none of which is a\\nsmall addition to the profile row. Building a fraction of it alongside the refund would\\nhave made both worse.\\n\\nCredit is also untouched: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and #75\\nowns money.\\n\\nSo what remains on this issue after slice 5 (end-to-end coverage):\\n\\n- Rebooking a disrupted itinerary onto another flight.\\n- Credit as an alternative to a refund, if it is wanted at all once #75 lands.\\n\\nBoth are worth their own issues rather than lingering here; happy to file them when the\\nslices are done, or fold rebooking into a Phase 4 customer-support item, whichever you\\nprefer.\\n\\n## One deliberate gap in the disruption path\\n\\n`lib/PointsActivityService.ts` keys every branch off `=== 'CANCELLED'`, so a `DISRUPTED`\\nbooking still earns status points and still shows as an ordinary credit. That is the\\nright behaviour — the customer has not cancelled and may yet fly a rebooked itinerary —\\nbut nothing tests it, and it will need revisiting when rebooking lands.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T18:30:30Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5244346371\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true},{\"id\":\"IC_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAABOKZr9A\",\"author\":{\"login\":\"scottdensmore\"},\"authorAssociation\":\"OWNER\",\"body\":\"## All five slices are merged\\n\\n| Slice | PR | What it did |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | #228 | `Booking.status` typed as an enum; an append-only status history that writes itself |\\n| 2 | #230 | Seats released by saying so, not by overwriting the seat number |\\n| 3 | #231 | The cancellation policy: eligibility, fees, refunds recorded |\\n| 4 | #232 | A staff flight cancellation disrupts its bookings, and can be undone |\\n| 5 | #233 | Browser journeys for the self-service and staff-assisted paths |\\n\\nAgainst this issue's original task list:\\n\\n- **Cancellation eligibility by fare and departure time** — done. Refused once departed;\\n  First and Business refund in full before the 24-hour cut-off, Premium Economy and\\n  Economy less a fifth; inside the cut-off cancellation is still allowed at nothing back.\\n- **Release seats without rewriting seat identifiers** — done. A partial unique index\\n  covers held seats only, so a released row keeps the seat the traveller actually sat in.\\n- **Calculate and record refunds** — done, on the history row, via a transaction-local\\n  channel. No money moves: #75 owns that and will have a figure to refund against.\\n- **Handle airline-initiated cancellations** — done, as `DISRUPTED`, reversibly.\\n- **Preserve immutable booking and status history** — done, enforced by a trigger rather\\n  than by convention.\\n- **Offer rebooking, refund or credit** — the refund only. Rebooking is now #234; credit\\n  is folded in there and probably depends on #75.\\n\\nAcceptance criteria: the changes are transactional and auditable, customer, inventory and\\nnotification states stay consistent, and the two journeys are covered end to end.\\n\\n## Worth knowing before this is called finished\\n\\n**#84 undercuts the departure guard.** `Flight.departureDate` is local wall-clock time\\nlabelled UTC, so east of UTC a departed flight stays cancellable for the length of the\\norigin's offset — the exact case slice 3 closes. The policy is exact against the value it\\nis given; the value is not. Recorded on #84.\\n\\n**#229 and #208 together hide this feature on a phone.** The Status column is scrolled out\\nof the visible area and the notification drawer renders behind the page, so a disrupted\\nbooking looks identical to a confirmed one and the fallback channel is unreadable.\\nNeither is caused by this work; both are now load-bearing for it.\\n\\nLeaving this open rather than closing it, since #234 came out of it and the milestone is\\nyours to call.\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T20:09:40Z\",\"includesCreatedEdit\":false,\"isMinimized\":false,\"minimizedReason\":\"\",\"reactionGroups\":[],\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76#issuecomment-5245398004\",\"viewerDidAuthor\":true}],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":76,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.4 Implement cancellation, disruption, and refund rules\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/76\"}\n\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"ISSUE 77\n{\"body\":\"Migrated from `REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md`.\\n\\n## Tasks\\n\\n- [ ] Add check-in eligibility windows and restrictions.\\n- [ ] Confirm traveler/document details at check-in.\\n- [ ] Support seat confirmation or allowed seat changes.\\n- [ ] Generate a boarding pass for each eligible traveler and leg.\\n- [ ] Provide confirmation numbers, receipts, and resend actions.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- The Check-In navigation leads to a complete journey.\\n- Ineligible customers receive a clear reason and next step.\\n- Boarding passes are scoped to the authenticated booking owner.\\n\",\"comments\":[],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":{\"number\":3,\"title\":\"Phase 2\",\"description\":\"Real booking lifecycle: replace demo booking and payment behavior with explicit, auditable domain workflows.\",\"dueOn\":null},\"number\":77,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"P2.5 Implement check-in and travel documents\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/77\"}\n\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"ISSUE 234\n{\"body\":\"Split out of #76, which delivered the refund half of \\\"offer rebooking, refund, or credit\\nfor disrupted itineraries\\\" across five slices. Rebooking was deliberately left out rather\\nthan half-built.\\n\\n## Where things stand\\n\\nA flight cancelled by staff moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED` (#232). The seat stays\\nheld, the customer sees the state on their profile, and their only offered action is to\\ncancel for a full refund with no fee (#231). Reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\nSo a customer who wants to travel anyway has no path: they must take the refund and book\\nagain from scratch, losing their seat and paying whatever the fare is by then.\\n\\n## What rebooking needs\\n\\n- A flight search constrained to the same route and a comparable date, offered from the\\n  disrupted booking rather than from the search page.\\n- Seat reselection on the new flight — the held seat does not transfer, and the new\\n  aircraft may have a different layout (`lib/seatLayout.ts`).\\n- A fare difference to resolve. The original `totalPriceCents` was charged; the\\n  replacement may cost more or less, and #75 owns whether anything moves.\\n- A decision about the original booking's history: is a rebooking a new `Booking` with\\n  the old one cancelled, or the same booking repointed at a different flight? The second\\n  keeps the reference and the passenger records but makes `ItineraryLeg` mutable, which\\n  nothing else in the schema currently allows.\\n- What it does to `releasedAt` on the old leg, and to the status history — `#231` added\\n  `BookingStatusChange.refundCents` and a monotonic `sequence`, so there is somewhere to\\n  record it.\\n\\nThat last question is the one to settle first; the rest follows from it.\\n\\n## Credit\\n\\nAlso unbuilt, and further out: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and\\nwhether it is wanted at all probably depends on what #75 does with payments.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #76 — the parent, and the decision record for the cancellation policy\\n- #75 — real payments, which owns any money actually moving\",\"comments\":[],\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"milestone\":null,\"number\":234,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Rebook a disrupted itinerary instead of refunding it\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/234\"}\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a00bb2-2acf-7d53-ab0c-ded966187af9"}}}
