{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T17:58:55.832Z","ordinal":23678,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"01a00bb5-84d2-75b2-a295-9f36c2da7b8e","last_agent_message":"The prototype changed my initial recommendation: we should not create a second booking.\n\nThe right model is:\n\n- Keep the existing `Booking`, confirmation reference, passengers, and captured payment.\n- Retain the disrupted `ItineraryLeg` and its released seat as history.\n- Create a replacement leg linked to the original.\n- Mark only the replaced leg as superseded; unaffected round-trip legs retain their identity and seats.\n- Record an immutable `BookingRebooking` event mapping old legs to replacement legs.\n- Return the booking from `DISRUPTED` to `CONFIRMED` only after every cancelled active leg is resolved.\n\nFor airline-caused disruption, fare differences should be waived for a same-route, same-cabin replacement. No extra charge or partial refund occurs; the existing full-refund option remains available. This avoids splitting the booking across multiple payments while giving the customer a straightforward choice.\n\nThe executable prototype compared all three approaches:\n\n| Model | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Mutate existing legs | Loses original itinerary and seat history |\n| Create another booking | Splits reference, passenger PII, payment, and receipt ownership |\n| Version legs within the booking | Preserves all eight tested invariants |\n\nIt also passed cheaper, equal, higher-fare, and round-trip replacement scenarios. The throwaway prototype is at [rebooking-model-prototype.mjs](/tmp/rebooking-model-prototype.mjs).\n\nI recommend two implementation slices:\n\n1. Behavior-neutral itinerary-versioning foundation and migration.\n2. Complete self-service rebooking with candidate search, seat reselection, transactional replacement, accessible UI, and E2E coverage.\n\nThe repository remains untouched and clean on `main`.","started_at":1786902840,"completed_at":1786903135,"duration_ms":295289,"time_to_first_token_ms":27324}}
