{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T18:02:50.948Z","ordinal":23791,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"019ffe5b-1054-7c22-8afc-e97cb84c7b73","turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-9228f0f5-eef3-46fd-91d3-86b214d9207d","process_id":"75954","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","sed -n '275,290p' prisma/schema.prisma\nsed -n '455,490p' prisma/schema.prisma"],"cwd":"file:///home/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/travel-app","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '275,290p' prisma/schema.prisma","name":"schema.prisma","path":"prisma/schema.prisma"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '455,490p' prisma/schema.prisma","name":"schema.prisma","path":"prisma/schema.prisma"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"  sensitiveDataDeletedAt  DateTime?\n  gender                  String\n\n  bookingId Int\n  booking   Booking @relation(fields: [bookingId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)\n\n  /// Where this traveller sits: one per leg. A traveller is a person; a seat\n  /// belongs to a flight, so the two are separate records (#137).\n  seatAssignments SeatAssignment[]\n\n  @@index([sensitiveDataExpiresAt])\n}\n\n/// Whether a flight is running.\n///\n/// Typed in the database rather than left as free text: the column was\n/// anything the moment it expires (#74).\n///\n/// Nothing sweeps these. An abandoned checkout costs the airline nothing\n/// because the row stops being live on its own, so there is no job whose\n/// failure would quietly reduce inventory -- which is the acceptance criterion\n/// this shape is chosen for.\nmodel SeatHold {\n  id         String   @id\n  flightId   Int\n  flight     Flight   @relation(fields: [flightId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)\n  seatNumber String\n  /// One server-derived user + checkout identity. This is not a relation: the\n  /// checkout UUID scopes two tabs for one account independently.\n  holderKey  String\n  /// The migration writes `DEFAULT now()`, so the database supplies this. No\n  /// production path calls `prisma.seatHold.create` -- the claim is one raw\n  /// `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT` -- and the `expiresAt > createdAt` check would\n  /// reject a row whose two times came from clocks that disagreed.\n  createdAt  DateTime @default(now()) @db.Timestamptz(3)\n  expiresAt  DateTime @db.Timestamptz(3)\n\n  /// One live claim per seat. Expired rows stay in the table and are taken\n  /// over in place, so this is unconditional rather than partial: a second row\n  /// for the same seat should never exist even when the first is dead.\n  @@unique([flightId, seatNumber])\n  @@index([expiresAt])\n  @@index([holderKey])\n}\n\n/// Where one traveller sits on one leg. Separated from Passenger because a\n/// traveller is one person per booking while a seat is one per leg: a round trip\n/// would otherwise need a second Passenger row, duplicating that person's\n/// encrypted passport and date of birth along with their retention deadlines.\n///\n/// flightId is denormalised from the leg so that the seat-uniqueness constraint\n/// can be expressed directly. A composite foreign key onto (id, flightId) of the\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"  sensitiveDataDeletedAt  DateTime?\n  gender                  String\n\n  bookingId Int\n  booking   Booking @relation(fields: [bookingId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)\n\n  /// Where this traveller sits: one per leg. A traveller is a person; a seat\n  /// belongs to a flight, so the two are separate records (#137).\n  seatAssignments SeatAssignment[]\n\n  @@index([sensitiveDataExpiresAt])\n}\n\n/// Whether a flight is running.\n///\n/// Typed in the database rather than left as free text: the column was\n/// anything the moment it expires (#74).\n///\n/// Nothing sweeps these. An abandoned checkout costs the airline nothing\n/// because the row stops being live on its own, so there is no job whose\n/// failure would quietly reduce inventory -- which is t