{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T18:15:31.782Z","ordinal":24103,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_00fb20eee1c75140016a81fe41fd148198a33cc16be4dfe796","status":"completed","call_id":"call_BsyeZtOChLPRNYtUTR2lcSEq","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"gh issue view 234 --json number,title,body,url,labels,state\",\"workdir\":\"/home/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/travel-app\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":12000,\"sandbox_permissions\":\"require_escalated\",\"justification\":\"May I refresh the current GitHub issue #234 acceptance criteria to keep this foundation slice aligned with the live roadmap issue?\",\"prefix_rule\":[\"gh\",\"issue\",\"view\"]});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T18:15:34.875Z","ordinal":24104,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"019ffe5b-1054-7c22-8afc-e97cb84c7b73","turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-da49fae6-aee1-439c-aabe-6479996ae3d7","process_id":"13207","command":["/bin/bash","-lc","gh issue view 234 --json number,title,body,url,labels,state"],"cwd":"file:///home/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/travel-app","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh issue view 234 --json number,title,body,url,labels,state"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"body\":\"Split out of #76, which delivered the refund half of \\\"offer rebooking, refund, or credit\\nfor disrupted itineraries\\\" across five slices. Rebooking was deliberately left out rather\\nthan half-built.\\n\\n## Where things stand\\n\\nA flight cancelled by staff moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED` (#232). The seat stays\\nheld, the customer sees the state on their profile, and their only offered action is to\\ncancel for a full refund with no fee (#231). Reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\nSo a customer who wants to travel anyway has no path: they must take the refund and book\\nagain from scratch, losing their seat and paying whatever the fare is by then.\\n\\n## What rebooking needs\\n\\n- A flight search constrained to the same route and a comparable date, offered from the\\n  disrupted booking rather than from the search page.\\n- Seat reselection on the new flight — the held seat does not transfer, and the new\\n  aircraft may have a different layout (`lib/seatLayout.ts`).\\n- A fare difference to resolve. The original `totalPriceCents` was charged; the\\n  replacement may cost more or less, and #75 owns whether anything moves.\\n- A decision about the original booking's history: is a rebooking a new `Booking` with\\n  the old one cancelled, or the same booking repointed at a different flight? The second\\n  keeps the reference and the passenger records but makes `ItineraryLeg` mutable, which\\n  nothing else in the schema currently allows.\\n- What it does to `releasedAt` on the old leg, and to the status history — `#231` added\\n  `BookingStatusChange.refundCents` and a monotonic `sequence`, so there is somewhere to\\n  record it.\\n\\nThat last question is the one to settle first; the rest follows from it.\\n\\n## Credit\\n\\nAlso unbuilt, and further out: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and\\nwhether it is wanted at all probably depends on what #75 does with payments.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #76 — the parent, and the decision record for the cancellation policy\\n- #75 — real payments, which owns any money actually moving\",\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"number\":234,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Rebook a disrupted itinerary instead of refunding it\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/234\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"body\":\"Split out of #76, which delivered the refund half of \\\"offer rebooking, refund, or credit\\nfor disrupted itineraries\\\" across five slices. Rebooking was deliberately left out rather\\nthan half-built.\\n\\n## Where things stand\\n\\nA flight cancelled by staff moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED` (#232). The seat stays\\nheld, the customer sees the state on their profile, and their only offered action is to\\ncancel for a full refund with no fee (#231). Reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\nSo a customer who wants to travel anyway has no path: they must take the refund and book\\nagain from scratch, losing their seat and paying whatever the fare is by then.\\n\\n## What rebooking needs\\n\\n- A flight search constrained to the same route and a comparable date, offered from the\\n  disrupted booking rather than from the search page.\\n- Seat reselection on the new flight — the held seat does not transfer, and the new\\n  aircraft may have a different layout (`lib/seatLayout.ts`).\\n- A fare difference to resolve. The original `totalPriceCents` was charged; the\\n  replacement may cost more or less, and #75 owns whether anything moves.\\n- A decision about the original booking's history: is a rebooking a new `Booking` with\\n  the old one cancelled, or the same booking repointed at a different flight? The second\\n  keeps the reference and the passenger records but makes `ItineraryLeg` mutable, which\\n  nothing else in the schema currently allows.\\n- What it does to `releasedAt` on the old leg, and to the status history — `#231` added\\n  `BookingStatusChange.refundCents` and a monotonic `sequence`, so there is somewhere to\\n  record it.\\n\\nThat last question is the one to settle first; the rest follows from it.\\n\\n## Credit\\n\\nAlso unbuilt, and further out: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and\\nwhether it is wanted at all probably depends on what #75 does with payments.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #76 — the parent, and the decision record for the cancellation policy\\n- #75 — real payments, which owns any money actually moving\",\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"number\":234,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Rebook a disrupted itinerary instead of refunding it\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/234\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":399808867},"formatted_output":"{\"body\":\"Split out of #76, which delivered the refund half of \\\"offer rebooking, refund, or credit\\nfor disrupted itineraries\\\" across five slices. Rebooking was deliberately left out rather\\nthan half-built.\\n\\n## Where things stand\\n\\nA flight cancelled by staff moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED` (#232). The seat stays\\nheld, the customer sees the state on their profile, and their only offered action is to\\ncancel for a full refund with no fee (#231). Reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\nSo a customer who wants to travel anyway has no path: they must take the refund and book\\nagain from scratch, losing their seat and paying whatever the fare is by then.\\n\\n## What rebooking needs\\n\\n- A flight search constrained to the same route and a comparable date, offered from the\\n  disrupted booking rather than from the search page.\\n- Seat reselection on the new flight — the held seat does not transfer, and the new\\n  aircraft may have a different layout (`lib/seatLayout.ts`).\\n- A fare difference to resolve. The original `totalPriceCents` was charged; the\\n  replacement may cost more or less, and #75 owns whether anything moves.\\n- A decision about the original booking's history: is a rebooking a new `Booking` with\\n  the old one cancelled, or the same booking repointed at a different flight? The second\\n  keeps the reference and the passenger records but makes `ItineraryLeg` mutable, which\\n  nothing else in the schema currently allows.\\n- What it does to `releasedAt` on the old leg, and to the status history — `#231` added\\n  `BookingStatusChange.refundCents` and a monotonic `sequence`, so there is somewhere to\\n  record it.\\n\\nThat last question is the one to settle first; the rest follows from it.\\n\\n## Credit\\n\\nAlso unbuilt, and further out: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and\\nwhether it is wanted at all probably depends on what #75 does with payments.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #76 — the parent, and the decision record for the cancellation policy\\n- #75 — real payments, which owns any money actually moving\",\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"number\":234,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Rebook a disrupted itinerary instead of refunding it\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/234\"}\n"},"started_at_ms":1786904134475,"completed_at_ms":1786904134875}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T18:15:35.052Z","ordinal":24105,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a00bc9-458c-7ad1-8430-c14f33eae8bf","call_id":"call_BsyeZtOChLPRNYtUTR2lcSEq","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"body\":\"Split out of #76, which delivered the refund half of \\\"offer rebooking, refund, or credit\\nfor disrupted itineraries\\\" across five slices. Rebooking was deliberately left out rather\\nthan half-built.\\n\\n## Where things stand\\n\\nA flight cancelled by staff moves its bookings to `DISRUPTED` (#232). The seat stays\\nheld, the customer sees the state on their profile, and their only offered action is to\\ncancel for a full refund with no fee (#231). Reinstating the flight puts them back.\\n\\nSo a customer who wants to travel anyway has no path: they must take the refund and book\\nagain from scratch, losing their seat and paying whatever the fare is by then.\\n\\n## What rebooking needs\\n\\n- A flight search constrained to the same route and a comparable date, offered from the\\n  disrupted booking rather than from the search page.\\n- Seat reselection on the new flight — the held seat does not transfer, and the new\\n  aircraft may have a different layout (`lib/seatLayout.ts`).\\n- A fare difference to resolve. The original `totalPriceCents` was charged; the\\n  replacement may cost more or less, and #75 owns whether anything moves.\\n- A decision about the original booking's history: is a rebooking a new `Booking` with\\n  the old one cancelled, or the same booking repointed at a different flight? The second\\n  keeps the reference and the passenger records but makes `ItineraryLeg` mutable, which\\n  nothing else in the schema currently allows.\\n- What it does to `releasedAt` on the old leg, and to the status history — `#231` added\\n  `BookingStatusChange.refundCents` and a monotonic `sequence`, so there is somewhere to\\n  record it.\\n\\nThat last question is the one to settle first; the rest follows from it.\\n\\n## Credit\\n\\nAlso unbuilt, and further out: there is no credit balance anywhere in the schema, and\\nwhether it is wanted at all probably depends on what #75 does with payments.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- #76 — the parent, and the decision record for the cancellation policy\\n- #75 — real payments, which owns any money actually moving\",\"labels\":[{\"id\":\"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAACtc5f4A\",\"name\":\"roadmap\",\"description\":\"Product hardening work migrated from REAL_WORLD_ROADMAP.md\",\"color\":\"5319e7\"}],\"number\":234,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Rebook a disrupted itinerary instead of refunding it\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/scottdensmore/travel-app/issues/234\"}\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1"}}}
