{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T19:29:38.430Z","ordinal":25614,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a00c0d-127e-7711-a203-cfe42e232e1d","author":"/root/verifier","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/verifier\nPayload:\nVerifier report for `feat/234-itinerary-versioning`: not ready to pass.\n\nFindings\n\n1. High — the supposedly immutable rebooking audit can be rewritten.\n\n   In a populated, freshly migrated scratch database, I created a valid rebooking, deleted its `BookingRebooking` row, then reinserted the event and mapping with `createdAt = 2000-01-01`. The transaction committed and readback returned that forged timestamp.\n\n   The deferred preservation trigger only verifies that a mapping exists at commit, while the append-only triggers prohibit `UPDATE` but not delete-and-reinsert. Mutant rows 11, 12, and 23 do not attack this path.\n\n2. Medium — the full database project deterministically fails.\n\n   Result: 30/33 suites passed; 236/236 test bodies passed, but three suites failed during `afterAll`:\n\n   - `__tests__/lib/itineraryLegs.database.test.ts`\n   - `__tests__/lib/seatOccupancy.database.test.ts`\n   - `__tests__/lib/cabinClass.database.test.ts`\n\n   Their cleanup deletes `ItineraryLeg` while its booking still exists. The new `ItineraryLeg_deletes_with_booking` trigger correctly rejects that operation with `P0001`. An isolated rerun reproduced it. The test cleanup should delete the booking and exercise the intended cascade.\n\n3. Coverage gap — no focused assertion isolates the rebooking event’s `bookingId` consistency check. Row 8 is killed by status/refund scenarios, not by associating the event with the wrong booking.\n\n4. Coverage gap — no focused assertion isolates either mapping role invariant:\n\n   - `fromLeg` must already be superseded.\n   - `toLeg` must still be active.\n\n   Row 7 is killed by sequence and cross-booking cases, leaving these predicates independently unconstrained.\n\nChecks\n\n- `npx prisma generate`: passed.\n- `npx prisma validate`: passed.\n- `npx tsc --noEmit`: passed.\n- `npm run lint`: passed with the same 8 baseline warnings and no errors.\n- `npm run test:unit`: passed, 73 suites and 825/825 tests. Existing React `act(...)` warnings remain unrelated.\n- `npm run test:database`: failed as described above.\n- `npm run build`: passed, including standalone sanitization.\n- `npx playwright test`: passed 41/41 in 5.4 minutes; teardown was clean. One non-failing `ECONNRESET: aborted` server message occurred and did not reproduce as a journey failure.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n\nMigration proof\n\n- Fresh scratch: all 43 migrations deployed and seed completed.\n- Populated scratch: deployed the prior 42 migrations, seeded and inserted a legacy booking/leg, then applied the new migration successfully.\n- Readback confirmed the legacy leg remained active and the partial unique index applies only where `supersededAt IS NULL`.\n- The focused rebooking database suite passed 21/21 against the populated migrated database, covering the reported normal and guard rejection paths.\n- Both verifier scratch databases were dropped.\n- The audit-rewrite proof above demonstrates a guard path that currently passes when it should fail.\n\nMutation audit\n\nAll reported production mutants were restored.\n\n- Rows 1–5 directly constrain active filtering, lock filtering, partial uniqueness, valid rebooking, and active replacement creation.\n- Rows 6 and 24 duplicate the immutable-trigger attack; the strengthened tests constrain unsuperseding and identity changes.\n- Rows 7–10 constrain mapping/event consistency, zero-leg events, and recorded supersession broadly, subject to the two predicate gaps above.\n- Rows 11–12 constrain `UPDATE` append-only behavior but miss delete-and-reinsert.\n- Row 13 directly constrains booking cascade deletion.\n- Rows 14–22 constrain the active-leg reads and reverse-relation queries in actions, services, profile, and admin paths.\n- Row 23 constrains bare deletion but misses replacement within the same transaction.\n- Rows 25–26 constrain direct leg deletion and live-old-seat replacement.\n- Row 27 honestly records its initial survivor; the corrected historical-source deletion test constrains that FK direction.\n- Row 28 constrains the replacement-leg FK direction.\n- Row 29 is correctly classified as observed defect/fix evidence rather than a claimed mutant rerun; fresh migration and cascade tests constrain the restored pair.\n\nSelection otherwise covers payment/reference preservation, released old seats, live replacement seats, fare waiver, and flight-lock behavior.\n\nSkips\n\n- Production dependency audit: not applicable; dependency manifests are unchanged.\n- Container secret scan/probes: not applicable; Dockerfile, standalone output, and sanitizer paths are unchanged.\n\nIntegrity\n\n- No tracked or status-reported file was changed by verification.\n- Final status remains exactly the intended 11 modified and 3 untracked paths, with no staged or committed branch diff.\n- `.next` is absent.\n- Verifier scratch databases and leaked fixtures from the failed teardown were removed; leak readback was zero.\n- Port 3000 was free after Playwright teardown. A later sandbox-only `ss` read was denied netlink access, but no server was started afterward."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1"}}}
