{"timestamp":"2026-08-16T19:51:43.564Z","ordinal":25946,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a00c21-4acc-7022-8956-f72ee5402fad","author":"/root/verifier","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/verifier\nPayload:\nFinal verifier report for `feat/234-itinerary-versioning`: pass, with no remaining actionable findings.\n\nResults\n\n- `npx prisma generate`: sandboxed invocation silently exited without generating; permitted-path rerun generated Prisma Client 5.22.0 successfully.\n- `npx prisma validate`: passed.\n- `npx tsc --noEmit`: passed.\n- `npm run lint`: passed with 0 errors and the same 8 baseline warnings.\n- `npm run test:unit`: passed, 73 suites and 825/825 tests.\n- `npm run test:database`: passed, 33 suites and 239/239 tests.\n- `npm run build`:\n  - Sandboxed attempt failed because Turbopack was denied permission to bind its internal port.\n  - Required permitted-path rerun passed, including TypeScript, all routes, and standalone sanitization.\n- `npx playwright test`: passed 41/41 in 5.6 minutes; global teardown passed.\n\nThe full database result confirms all three repaired cleanup suites now pass and no longer conflict with the intentional direct-leg-delete guard.\n\nMigration proof\n\n- Fresh scratch database: all 43 migrations deployed and seed completed.\n- Populated scratch database:\n  - Deployed the 42 pre-slice migrations.\n  - Seeded successfully and inserted a representative legacy booking with an itinerary leg.\n  - Applied `20260816181000_version_itinerary_legs`.\n  - Readback confirmed the legacy leg remained active.\n  - Confirmed the exact partial unique index:\n    `(\"bookingId\", sequence) WHERE \"supersededAt\" IS NULL`.\n  - The complete rebooking integrity suite passed 24/24 against this populated migrated database.\n- The guard suite exercised successful rebooking and booking cascade paths plus the invalid guard paths, including rewritten-history replacement, wrong-booking events, and reversed mapping roles.\n- Both verifier scratch databases were dropped; final matching-database count was zero.\n\nMutation and selection audit\n\nAll 32 reported rows are restored and auditable.\n\n- Rows 1–5 constrain active filtering, lock filtering, partial uniqueness, valid rebooking, and new-leg activation.\n- Rows 6 and 24 constrain immutable leg identity and permanent supersession with the strengthened assertions.\n- Rows 7–10 constrain mapping consistency, status-event consistency, zero-leg events, and recorded supersession.\n- Rows 11–12 constrain append-only updates.\n- Row 13 and rows 27–29 cover both historical/replacement FK directions and whole-booking cascade behavior. Row 27 is correctly reported as an initial survivor followed by a corrected selection; row 29 remains observed defect/fix evidence rather than a claimed mutant rerun.\n- Rows 14–22 constrain every changed active-leg read and reverse-relation read in actions, services, profile, admin dashboard, and manifest paths.\n- Rows 23, 25, and 26 constrain direct audit deletion, direct leg deletion, and live old-seat refusal.\n- Row 30 directly attacks the former delete-and-reinsert bypass. The test forces the deferred constraint immediately, requires the exact deletion error, and verifies the original timestamp survives, so it is not vacuous.\n- Row 31 directly removes the event/status-change booking identity predicate and is constrained by the exact wrong-booking transaction.\n- Row 32 directly removes the active-destination predicate. The reversed-role test records the insertion error independently before forcing rollback, so later deferred guards cannot satisfy it accidentally.\n\nThe removed explicit historical-source predicate is logically redundant: mapping legs must differ, share booking and sequence, and the destination must be active; the immediate partial unique index permits only one active leg at that booking/sequence, so the distinct source is necessarily historical. Row 32 selects the remaining role-defining predicate.\n\nSelection also directly covers payment/reference preservation, fare waiver, released historical seats, live replacement seats, flight locking, and active-only rendered reads. I found no remaining vacuous assertion or uncovered scoped state-machine branch.\n\nClassification\n\n- Regression findings from the prior pass are resolved.\n- The sandboxed Prisma-generation and build failures were environment restrictions; both passed through the permitted path.\n- Playwright emitted recurring non-failing `ECONNRESET: aborted` server logs, including uncaught-exception logging, but no journey or teardown failed. This matches the earlier runtime noise and is not connected to this migration/test-only re-hand.\n- Existing React `act(...)` warnings in the unit project and the 8 lint warnings remain baseline/unrelated.\n\nSkipped\n\n- Production dependency audit: not applicable; package manifests are unchanged.\n- Container secret scan and rejection probes: not applicable; Dockerfile, standalone, and sanitizer sources are unchanged.\n- UI re-review: correctly not repeated because the finding-resolution edits affect only migration constraints and database-test cleanup; rendered product source is unchanged from the passed three-viewport review.\n\nFinal integrity\n\n- No status-reported file was changed by verification.\n- Final status is exactly 14 modified and 3 untracked intended paths, with no staged or committed branch diff.\n- `git diff --check` passes.\n- `.next` and the temporary Prisma copy are absent.\n- No verifier scratch database remains.\n- Playwright’s server stopped and port 3000 is free.\n- Pre-existing database and Mailpit containers remain running; no unrelated service was stopped."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a00bba-29dd-7100-8715-811d18e7c7e1"}}}
